\section{The activity: one launch per course}
\label{ViewLaunch}

A launch costs four requests and goes through one Canvas assignment
(\cref{Launch}), and one launch serves the whole course.
So the [[Viewer]] launches the first time it meets a course and keeps the
client, together with the map from placement codes to activities
(\cref{Canvas}).
It launches through the course's safest FBF assignment, as the commands
do, not through the assignment being viewed: viewing a submission of an
assignment that nobody has opened in this course round must not create its
activity.
That is why we list the course's assignments, from [[canvaslms]]'s cache,
instead of using the one at hand.
If that list holds no FBF assignment, as a stale cached list might, there
is nothing safe to launch through, and the course has no activities for us.

A launch that fails is remembered too, and raised again for the next
submission of the course.
When FBF is down, a view of a whole assignment would otherwise try the
launch, and wait for its timeouts, once per student.

An assignment whose code is not among the activities has no activity in
this round, and we say so rather than show nothing, since nothing would
look like a student who did nothing.
The course's activities may come from the cache, kept for days
(\cref{Cache}), and an activity opened since is not in them.
So a code we do not find makes us ask FBF once more for the course's
activities, once per course and run, before we conclude that there is no
activity.

The client is made by [[connect]], which the tests replace with one that
returns the fake client of \cref{fake-fbf}.

\subsection{Between runs}

A launch per course and run was the largest single cost of a view, over two
seconds (\cref{Cache}).
The [[Viewer]] therefore keeps one client for all courses, since one pair of
tokens serves them all, and starts it from the tokens of an earlier run,
kept in the keyring (\cref{CacheTokens}).
What the tokens do not say is the FBF course of a Canvas course, which only a
launch through that course tells; the cache file keeps that map, so a course
seen before needs no launch at all.
A course not seen before is launched, as before, and its launch also renews
the client's tokens.
If the stored tokens have expired, the client's own renewal refreshes them
or launches again (\cref{API}), through the course's safest assignment.

The cache file is read when the viewer is made and written by [[close]],
which the caller calls when it is done, together with the tokens.
A viewer made without a Canvas host and token, as in the tests, keeps its
cache in memory only and leaves the keyring alone.
With [[refresh]], for [[canvaslms --refresh-cache]], the viewer takes
nothing from earlier runs: the cache serves nothing, each course is
launched, which also brings new tokens and repairs a stale course map, and
files are downloaded again.

<<imports>>=
import functools
from fbf import canvas as fbfcanvas
from fbf import cache as cache_module
from fbf.api import FBF
from fbf.launch import launch as lti_launch
<<constants>>=
NOT_OPENED = ("Nobody has opened this assignment in FeedbackFruits in this "
              "course round, so there is nothing to show.")
<<classes>>=
class Viewer:
    """Fetch the FBF work of one student at a time.

    `canvas` is a `canvasapi.Canvas`; `canvas_url` and `canvas_token` are
    what the launch needs (see `fbf.launch.launch`).  A viewer keeps what
    is the same for many submissions: one launch per course, and per
    activity its tasks, participants and their email addresses.  `errors`
    are the exceptions that mean FBF failed or changed, to be reported.

    Between runs it keeps a cache file and the tokens, which `close`
    writes; with `refresh` it takes nothing from earlier runs.
    `cache_path` replaces
    the cache file's usual place, and `relaunch` the launch that renews
    stored tokens that cannot be refreshed (for the tests).
    """

    errors = ERRORS

    def __init__(self, canvas, canvas_url, canvas_token, connect=None,
                 refresh=False, cache_path=None, relaunch=lti_launch):
        self.canvas = canvas
        self.relauncher = relaunch
        self.canvas_url = canvas_url
        self.canvas_token = canvas_token
        self.cache_path = cache_path
        self.connect = connect or functools.partial(
            fbfcanvas.connect, canvas_url, canvas_token)
        self.persistent = bool(canvas_url and canvas_token)
        self.cache = (cache_module.load_cache(canvas_url, canvas_token,
                                              refresh, path=cache_path)
                      if self.persistent
                      else cache_module.Cache(refresh=refresh))
        self.client = None
        self.asked_again = set()
        self.courses = {}
        self.activities = {}
        self.students = {}
        self.emails = {}

    <<viewer methods>>
<<viewer methods>>=
def course(self, course_id):
    """Return the FBF client of a Canvas course and its activities by code.

    Raises the error of a failed launch, the same for every call.
    """
    if course_id not in self.courses:
        try:
            self.courses[course_id] = self.launch(course_id)
        except ERRORS as error:
            self.courses[course_id] = error
    found = self.courses[course_id]
    if isinstance(found, Exception):
        raise found
    return found

def launch(self, course_id):
    """Reach FBF for a Canvas course; return the client and activities."""
    assignments = list(self.canvas.get_course(course_id).get_assignments())
    course_uuid = self.cache.courses.get(str(course_id))
    if course_uuid is not None and not self.cache.refresh \
            and self.stored_client(assignments):
        return self.client, fbfcanvas.activities_by_code(self.client,
                                                         course_uuid)
    fbf = self.connect(assignments)
    if fbf is None:
        return None, {}
    fbf.cache = self.cache
    self.client = fbf
    self.cache.courses[str(course_id)] = fbf.login.course_uuid
    return fbf, fbfcanvas.activities_by_code(fbf, fbf.login.course_uuid)

def activities_after_miss(self, course_id):
    """Ask FBF again for a course's activities, once per course and run."""
    fbf, activities = self.course(course_id)
    course_uuid = self.cache.courses.get(str(course_id))
    if fbf is None or course_uuid is None or course_id in self.asked_again:
        return activities
    self.asked_again.add(course_id)
    self.cache.forget(f"v1/courses/{course_uuid}/activities")
    activities = fbfcanvas.activities_by_code(fbf, course_uuid)
    self.courses[course_id] = (fbf, activities)
    return activities

def stored_client(self, assignments):
    """Return the client, from stored tokens if need be, or `None`."""
    if self.client is None and self.persistent:
        login = cache_module.stored_login(self.canvas_url)
        order = fbfcanvas.launch_order(assignments)
        if login is not None and order:
            relaunch = functools.partial(self.relauncher, self.canvas_url,
                                         self.canvas_token,
                                         order[0].course_id, order[0].id)
            self.client = FBF(login, relaunch=relaunch, cache=self.cache)
    return self.client

def close(self):
    """Keep the cache and the tokens for the next run."""
    if not self.persistent:
        return
    if self.client is not None:
        cache_module.store_login(self.canvas_url, self.client.login)
    cache_module.save_cache(self.cache, self.canvas_url, self.canvas_token,
                            self.cache_path)

def activity(self, fbf, activity_id):
    """Return the `Activity` of `activity_id`, the same one every time."""
    if activity_id not in self.activities:
        self.activities[activity_id] = Activity(fbf, activity_id)
    return self.activities[activity_id]
@

An [[Activity]] holds what the parts read about one activity, each fetched
the first time a part asks for it and then kept: the tasks, the
participants' progresses, the participants themselves, the submissions,
the review allocations, the reflections and the discussion threads.
A part that is not shown, such as the reviews of a video activity, costs
nothing.

<<imports>>=
from fbf.schema import attribute, related_id, relationship, requires
from fbf.discussions import related_link
from fbf import progress as progress_module
from fbf import discussions as discussion_module
<<classes>>=
class Activity:
    """What the parts of a view read about one FBF activity, kept."""

    def __init__(self, fbf, id):
        self.fbf = fbf
        self.id = id

    @functools.cached_property
    def tasks(self):
        """The tasks, as a list."""
        return progress_module.activity_tasks(self.fbf, self.id)

    @functools.cached_property
    def task(self):
        """The tasks by type."""
        return {task["type"]: task for task in self.tasks}

    @functools.cached_property
    def progresses(self):
        """The progresses of the participants."""
        return progress_module.activity_progresses(self.fbf, self.id)

    @functools.cached_property
    def users(self):
        """The participants, user resources by id."""
        return self.fbf.get_by_ids(
            "v1/users",
            [related_id(progress, "actor") for progress in self.progresses])

    @functools.cached_property
    def submissions(self):
        """The work handed in to the submit task, by all participants."""
        return self.fbf.get(related_link(self.task[SUBMIT_TASK],
                                         "submissions")).resources

    @functools.cached_property
    def allocations(self):
        """The allocations of the review task, all reviewers' and reviewees'."""
        return review_module.task_allocations(self.fbf, self.task)

    @functools.cached_property
    def reflections(self):
        """The reflections of all participants."""
        return self.fbf.get(related_link(
            self.task[review_module.REFLECT_TASK], "reflections")).resources

    @functools.cached_property
    def threads(self):
        """The discussion threads on the activity's materials."""
        return discussion_module.activity_threads(self.fbf, self.id, "")
