\subsection{Remembering the student}

One request stood out when we timed a view with the cache in place: the
list of the activity's progresses, which took FBF 0.9 to 1.2 seconds for
the 177 progresses of our test course, against 0.05 seconds for one
progress by its id, with the same attributes.
The view needs the list for two things only: to find the student among the
participants, and the student's own progress for the grade.
Both answers are stable.
The FBF users who are a Canvas student stay so, and a participant's
progress keeps its id while its content changes.
So the viewer remembers them, in the cache file under paths of its own
([[fbf/students/]]\ldots, [[fbf/progress-ids/]]\ldots), kept as long as
other details and as personal as the rest.
A view of a student seen before then fetches the student's progress by
its id, and never the list.
The same holds for the allocations of the review task, the slowest list
once the progresses were gone: 0.7 to 1.2 seconds for 261 allocations, of
which a student has some four, against 0.07 seconds for one by its id.
FBF ignores [[filter[actor]]] and [[filter[allocable]]] on the list, which
we checked, so we cannot ask it for the student's share.
We remember the ids of the student's allocations instead and fetch those,
together.
Unlike the progresses, a student's allocations can change as a whole: a
teacher may reallocate the reviews, so we remember them for a day only
(\cref{Cache}), after which the next view reads the list again.
The progresses taken from the list are also kept as the answers to those
requests by id, so a view within five minutes asks for nothing.
What it may miss is a second identity created in FBF since, until the
details expire or [[--refresh-cache]].

<<constants>>=
STUDENT_PATH = "fbf/students/{course}/{user}"
PROGRESS_PATH = "fbf/progress-ids/{activity}/{user}"
ALLOCATION_PATH = "fbf/allocation-ids/{activity}/{user}"
<<viewer methods>>=
def users_of(self, activity, course_id, student):
    """Return the ids of the FBF users who are `student`, remembered."""
    path = STUDENT_PATH.format(course=course_id, user=student.id)
    known = self.remembered(path)
    allocations = self.remembered(
        ALLOCATION_PATH.format(activity=activity.id, user=student.id))
    self.prefetch(activity, progresses=not known,
                  allocations=not allocations)
    if known:
        return set(known)
    users = student_users(activity.fbf, activity.users, student, self.emails)
    if users:
        self.remember(path, sorted(users))
    return users

def progresses_of(self, activity, users, student):
    """Return the progresses of `users` in `activity`, by remembered ids."""
    path = PROGRESS_PATH.format(activity=activity.id, user=student.id)
    ids = self.remembered(path)
    if ids:
        return [activity.fbf.get(f"v1/progresses/{id}").resource
                for id in ids]
    mine = [progress for progress in activity.progresses
            if related_id(progress, "actor") in users]
    if mine:
        self.remember(path, [progress["id"] for progress in mine])
    for progress in mine:
        self.cache.store(f"v1/progresses/{progress['id']}", {},
                         {"data": progress})
    return mine

def allocations_of(self, activity, users, student):
    """Return the allocations of `users` in `activity`, by remembered ids."""
    path = ALLOCATION_PATH.format(activity=activity.id, user=student.id)
    ids = self.remembered(path)
    if ids:
        return [document.resource for document in activity.fbf.get_many(
            [(f"v1/assignments/allocations/{id}", {}) for id in ids])]
    mine = [allocation for allocation in activity.allocations
            if users & {review_module.person(allocation, "actor"),
                        review_module.person(allocation, "allocable")}]
    if mine:
        self.remember(path, [allocation["id"] for allocation in mine])
    for allocation in mine:
        self.cache.store(f"v1/assignments/allocations/{allocation['id']}",
                         {}, {"data": allocation})
    return mine

def remembered(self, path):
    """Return what the viewer remembered under `path`, or `None`."""
    document = self.cache.lookup(path, {})
    return None if document is None else document["data"]

def remember(self, path, value):
    """Remember `value` under `path`, in the cache file."""
    self.cache.store(path, {}, {"data": value})
