\section{The reviews}
\label{ViewReviews}

A student both receives reviews and gives them, and a teacher needs both:
the reviews received explain much of the student's own grade, the reviews
given are often part of it.
Both come from the allocations of the review task, the student being the
[[allocable]] in one case and the [[actor]] in the other.
The fetchers of \cref{Reviews} take a test of the allocations to keep, so
we pass one for each and pay only for the student's allocations, a handful
of requests instead of hundreds.
The list of allocations itself they take from the [[Activity]], which
fetches it once for all students.
The four fetches, reviews and feedback for each direction, do not depend on
one another, so they run at the same time, as the parts do
(\cref{ViewTogether}).
With the reviews come the ratings of them: those the student gave the
reviews received, and those the reviewees gave the student's reviews.

<<constants>>=
RECEIVED_TITLE = "Reviews received"
GIVEN_TITLE = "Reviews given"
<<imports>>=
from fbf import reviews as review_module
<<functions>>=
def review_sections(activity, users, allocations=None):
    """Return the `Section`s of the reviews that `users` got and gave.

    `allocations`, if given, are those to read; else the activity's.
    """
    roles = [(RECEIVED_TITLE, "allocable", "reviewer"),
             (GIVEN_TITLE, "actor", "reviewee")]
    if allocations is None:
        allocations = activity.allocations
    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(2 * len(roles)) as pool:
        fetched = [(title, other,
                    pool.submit(review_module.fetch_reviews, activity.fbf,
                                activity.task, "", keep=in_role(users, role),
                                allocations=allocations),
                    pool.submit(review_module.fetch_feedback, activity.fbf,
                                activity.task, "", keep=in_role(users, role),
                                allocations=allocations))
                   for title, role, other in roles]
        return [Section(title, reviews_text(reviews.result(),
                                            feedbacks.result(), other))
                for title, other, reviews, feedbacks in fetched]


def in_role(users, role):
    """Return a test of whether an allocation has one of `users` as `role`."""
    return lambda allocation: review_module.person(allocation, role) in users
@

The text groups everything by the other party, the reviewer of a review
received or the reviewee of a review given, since that is how a review
reads: one person's assessment, criterion by criterion, then the rating it
got.
The comments are folded onto one line each, as in the [[fbf reviews]]
output, so that the list stays a list.

<<functions>>=
def reviews_text(reviews, feedbacks, other):
    """Return the Markdown of `reviews` and `feedbacks`, by `other` party."""
    parties = list(dict.fromkeys(
        [getattr(review, other) for review in reviews]
        + [getattr(feedback, other) for feedback in feedbacks]))
    if not parties:
        return "None."
    lines = []
    for party in parties:
        lines.append(f"- {party or 'Unknown'}")
        for review in reviews:
            if getattr(review, other) == party:
                level = f": {review.level} ({review.score})" \
                    if review.level else ""
                lines.append(f"  - {review.criterion}{level}")
                lines += [f"    - {review_module.fold(comment.body)}"
                          for comment in review.comments]
        for feedback in feedbacks:
            if getattr(feedback, other) == party:
                body = review_module.fold(feedback.body)
                lines.append(f"  - Rating of the review: {feedback.rating}"
                             + (f", {body}" if body else ""))
    return "\n".join(lines)
@

