\section{Putting it together}
\label{ViewTogether}

Which parts an activity has depends on its tasks: a peer review has a
submit task, a review task and perhaps a reflect task; a video activity
has a multimedia task; every activity has a grade.
We show the parts that the tasks call for, in the order a teacher reads
them: what was handed in, what it earned, what others said about it, what
the student said about others, and the student's own words.

When the student is looked for, the viewer fetches the activity's lists at
the same time ([[prefetch]]): the progresses, if finding the student needs
them (\cref{ViewStudent}), and those of the parts the activity has, the
work handed in, the allocations and the reflections.
Fetched one after the other, by whoever needs them first, they were a
chain of round trips; together they cost one.
A list that fails here is left to its part, which asks again and reports
the error in its place.

<<constants>>=
PREFETCHED = [("submissions", SUBMIT_TASK),
              ("allocations", review_module.REVIEW_TASK),
              ("reflections", review_module.REFLECT_TASK)]
<<viewer methods>>=
def prefetch(self, activity, progresses=True, allocations=True):
    """Fetch the lists of `activity` that the view will read, together.

    The progresses only if `progresses`, the allocations if `allocations`.
    """
    names = ["progresses"] if progresses else []
    names += [name for name, task in PREFETCHED if task in activity.task
              and (allocations or name != "allocations")]
    def fetch(name):
        try:
            getattr(activity, name)
        except ERRORS:
            pass
    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(len(names)) as pool:
        list(pool.map(fetch, names))
@

Let's verify that the lists are fetched together: the fake world holds each
list request at a barrier until all four have come, which they never would
one after the other.

